// Practice quiz: Repositories, derived queries and @Query
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/spring-data-jpa/repositories-derived-queries-and-at-query

::o1 inherited::Which method do you get just by extending JpaRepository? {
=findById#Correct. CRUD methods are inherited.
~findByTitle#Not quite. That is a derived method you declare.
~getConnection#Not quite. That is JDBC.
~executeQuery#Not quite. That is JDBC too.
}

::o2 derived name::What query does findByCapacityGreaterThan(int n) represent? {
=Rows where capacity is greater than n#Correct. The keyword maps to the comparison.
~Rows where capacity equals n#Not quite. Equality has no keyword.
~The row with the greatest capacity#Not quite. That would need a Top or First form.
~A count of matching rows#Not quite. That would start with countBy.
}

::o2 ignore case::Which method finds titles containing text regardless of letter case? {
=findByTitleContainingIgnoreCase#Correct. Both keywords are needed.
~findByTitle#Not quite. That is an exact, case-sensitive match on most databases.
~findByTitleLike#Not quite. That needs your own wildcards and is case-sensitive.
~findByIgnoreCaseTitle#Not quite. The keyword order is wrong.
}

::o3 JPQL target::What does a JPQL query refer to? {
=Entity classes and their fields#Correct. It is an object-level query language.
~Only table and column names#Not quite. That is native SQL.
~Java method names#Not quite. Method names are not part of JPQL.
~JSON fields#Not quite. JPQL has nothing to do with JSON here.
}

::o3 parameters::Why use a named parameter in @Query instead of joining strings? {
=It keeps user text out of the query string, preventing injection#Correct. The value is bound separately from the query.
~It makes the query longer#Not quite. Length is not the goal.
~It is required for every query#Not quite. A query may have no parameters.
~It enables caching of secrets#Not quite. That is unrelated.
}

